    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d694">

                <pb facs="0741" n="639" xml:id="pg639"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/1738: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-06-14T13:00:00+08:00">June 14, 1933—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 14—5:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>526. Department’s 181, May 31, 4 p.m.; 189, June 5, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d694-f1"/> and 193, June 10, 3 p.m.</p>
                <p>I instructed Peck to leave <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> with
                    Ministry of Foreign Affairs setting forth opinion expressed in last sentence
                    Department’s 193, June 10, 3 p.m., and also to inform Ministry of Railways that
                    American Government desired that treatment be accorded to Andersen, Meyer and
                    Company no less favorable than that accorded British creditors of the
                    Tientsin-Pukow Railway. To these instructions Peck has replied as follows:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I respectfully suggest that since the Legation is now making a special
                            effort to obtain favorable action on claims commission proposal the
                            railway debts be not pressed for a few days. Our insistence on treatment
                            similar to that accorded British provides opponents with argument
                            against concluding claims commission agreement with the United States
                            alone instead of waiting until all foreign debts may be dealt with
                            simultaneously.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I concur in Peck’s opinion and have instructed him to defer taking action until
                    reply has been received to proposal of establishment claims commission.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
